@@ -124,6 +128,26 @@ ### Call the next ticket
 implementation and database transaction layer; the domain invariant is that a
 ticket can be called only once.
 
+`CallNextTicket` implements this with one transaction: `lockForUpdate()` on the
+queue row (which also serializes against `IssueTicket` issuance/deactivation and
+forces a fresh `is_active` read, so a stale queue instance cannot authorize the
+call), deterministic candidate ordering (`priority DESC, issued_at ASC, id ASC`;
+normals-only path ordered `issued_at ASC, id ASC`), the 3-ticket fairness check
+(last 3 `called` rows by `called_at DESC, id DESC`; restrict to oldest waiting
+normal when all 3 are priority and a normal waits, `Any` mode only), then a
+per-candidate `lockForUpdate()` + `waiting` re-check so a ticket concurrently
+claimed via `Ticket::call()` (which locks only the ticket row) is skipped for
+the next candidate. The winner is saved with `status = called`,
+`called_at = now()`, `called_by_user_id` (write-once fields only) and returned
+refreshed. Failures are `InactiveQueueException` (inactive queue, nothing
+changed) and `NoWaitingTicketsException` (empty queue or no ticket eligible for
+the mode); both extend `RuntimeException`. Authorization (who may call) stays
+outside this action — the caller is trusted.
+
+Covered by `tests/Feature/CallNextTicketTest.php`: ordering, queue scoping,
+all three modes, fairness trigger/reset/history-scope/empty-normal cases, stale
+inactive-queue rejection, and the called-marker/return-value contract.
+
