 ## MVP Scope
 
@@ -98,7 +98,7 @@ ### Ticket
 
 **Immutability enforcement:** the write-once rule is enforced on the `Ticket` model (`updating` hook), so it applies to Eloquent saves only — mass updates through the query builder (`Ticket::query()->update(...)`) bypass it and must not be used on issued tickets.
 
-**Single-ticket call (scope note):** `Ticket::call()` marks one waiting ticket as `called` (entity-membership checked, exactly-once). This minimal domain primitive proves the mutable `status`/`called_*` fields; deterministic call-next selection (priority + age + 3-ticket fairness) and HTTP/Livewire panels remain out of scope per #14.
+**Deterministic call-next (implemented):** `App\Actions\CallNextTicket::__invoke(Queue $queue, User $calledBy, CallMode $mode = CallMode::Any)` selects the next waiting ticket of the queue and marks it `called` (`called_at = now()`, `called_by_user_id`), returning the updated ticket. Modes map to the attendant buttons: `CallMode::Any` = **Next** (full ordering `priority DESC, issued_at ASC, id ASC` plus the fairness rule below), `CallMode::PriorityOnly` = **Priority** (only `priority > 0`, same ordering, fairness ignored), `CallMode::NormalOnly` = **Normal** (only `priority = 0`, oldest first, fairness ignored). A ticket is **normal** when `priority = 0` and **priority** when `priority > 0` (higher = more important). Fairness (`Any` only): when the last 3 called tickets in the queue (most recent first by `called_at`, then `id`) are all priority tickets and at least one normal ticket is waiting, the call is restricted to the oldest waiting normal; calls made in any mode count in that history, a normal call resets the streak, and priority tickets keep being called when no normal is waiting. Only `waiting` tickets of the given queue are candidates (never another queue's, never an already-called ticket). The action runs in a transaction: it locks the queue row and re-reads `is_active` from the database (a stale passed instance never authorizes the call), then locks each candidate row and re-checks `waiting` before marking it — a ticket concurrently called through `Ticket::call()` is skipped for the next candidate. Inactive queues throw `App\Exceptions\InactiveQueueException` (changes nothing); no eligible ticket for the mode throws `App\Exceptions\NoWaitingTicketsException`. Only `status`, `called_at`, `called_by_user_id` change, respecting the write-once rule. No authorization inside the action (the caller is trusted).
 
 ### Priority
 
